# DevSecOps

Security is part of design, implementation, testing, integration and release.
It is not a final review phase.

Exact security tools and commands are documented in
[tooling.md](tooling.md). A public vulnerability-reporting process, when
needed, belongs in the repository’s `SECURITY.md`.

## Principles

- Use secure defaults.
- Apply least privilege to people, processes, credentials and services.
- Identify trust boundaries and validate data crossing them.
- Fail closed when continuing could weaken security or expose or corrupt data.
- Keep security decisions explicit and reviewable.
- Use established security libraries and platform capabilities.
- Do not implement custom cryptography, authentication or secret storage.
- Automate repeatable controls with established tools.
- Do not weaken a security control for development or testing convenience.
- Apply controls proportionally to the project’s actual exposure and data.

## Secrets and credentials

- Never commit credentials, tokens, passwords, private keys, certificates,
  private endpoints or realistic secret-shaped examples.
- Keep `.env` files and local credential files outside version control.
- Use `.env.example` only for variable names and safe synthetic values.
- Never place secrets in command-line arguments when they may appear in process
  listings or logs.
- Never print secrets, authorization headers, session values or private keys.
- Redact sensitive values before logging errors or external payloads.
- Use an approved secret provider for deployed environments.
- Scope credentials to the minimum permissions and lifetime required.
- Separate development, test, CI and production credentials.
- Never expose production credentials to pull-request code.
- Treat a committed or logged secret as compromised and rotate it.

## Untrusted input and sensitive data

- Treat data from users, files, networks, environment variables and external
  services as untrusted until validated.
- Validate type, format, size and allowed values at the owning boundary.
- Normalize input only after validation requirements are understood.
- Use parameterized platform APIs instead of constructing commands or queries
  through string concatenation.
- Never execute untrusted input as code or shell commands.
- Do not use unsafe deserialization for untrusted data.
- Minimize collection, storage and logging of sensitive data.
- Use synthetic data in tests and examples.
- Define retention, deletion, encryption and access requirements when the
  project handles sensitive or regulated data.
- Do not expose internal errors, stack traces, queries or sensitive identifiers
  through public responses.

## Threat review

Perform a focused threat review when a change introduces or materially changes:

- a trust boundary;
- authentication or authorization;
- sensitive or regulated data;
- externally reachable input;
- file or command execution;
- privileged operations;
- cryptography or credential handling;
- a third-party integration;
- deployment or release authority.

The review should identify assets, entry points, credible abuse cases, controls
and remaining risk. Do not require formal threat-model ceremony for changes
without a meaningful security boundary.

## Dependencies and supply chain

- Prefer the standard library and existing dependencies.
- Add a dependency only for a concrete requirement.
- Review its source, maintenance status, license and security posture.
- Declare direct dependencies explicitly and commit the native lockfile.
- Audit dependencies using an established vulnerability database and scanner.
- Remove unused dependencies.
- Pin CI actions, build images and downloaded tools to immutable versions when
  the platform supports it.
- Verify integrity for downloaded release or build artifacts.
- Do not execute unreviewed install scripts or remote scripts.
- Do not accept a dependency merely because an automated scanner currently
  reports no vulnerability.
- Generate provenance, an SBOM or signed artifacts only when the release profile
  requires them.

## CI and automation

- Give CI jobs the minimum permissions required.
- Keep default repository permissions read-only where supported.
- Grant write permissions only to the specific job that requires them.
- Do not expose secrets to code from untrusted forks.
- Do not interpolate untrusted event data directly into shell commands.
- Keep pull-request validation separate from privileged release jobs.
- Use immutable references for third-party CI components.
- Use check mode in CI; validation jobs must not modify source files.
- Keep logs free of secrets and unnecessary sensitive payloads.
- Preserve security scan results as bounded evidence without publishing
  sensitive findings.
- Never bypass a required security job to obtain a passing build.

## Security findings

- Treat verified exposed secrets as immediately actionable.
- Block completion for unresolved findings prohibited by the project’s approved
  severity policy.
- Fix valid findings at their cause.
- Do not suppress a finding without reviewing it.
- Keep every suppression narrow and identify the exact rule and location.
- A risk acceptance must state the reason, affected scope, compensating control,
  owner and expiration.
- Remove expired or obsolete suppressions.
- Do not lower scanner sensitivity merely to reduce findings.

## Security evidence

Use existing tool and platform output to measure security progress according to
[NORTHSTAR.md](../../NORTHSTAR.md).

Useful evidence includes:

- verified secret findings;
- dependency vulnerabilities by severity;
- static security findings;
- insecure CI configuration;
- unresolved risk acceptances;
- remediation time;
- security regressions and incidents.

Do not create a custom security dashboard until existing reports are
insufficient for an actual decision.

